# =============================================================================
# Second Brain — .gitignore
#
# Rule of thumb: ignore only what is (a) specific to ONE machine, (b) rewritten
# constantly by Obsidian, or (c) not yours to publish. Everything else — your
# notes AND your vault config — is committed, so cloning the repo on a new
# machine gives you an identically-configured vault.
#
# Deliberately NOT ignored: .obsidian/app.json, appearance.json,
# daily-notes.json, templates.json, hotkeys.json, core-plugins.json,
# community-plugins.json, themes/, snippets/, and every plugin's data.json.
# Those ARE the setup. See the plugin section below for the one nuance.
# =============================================================================

# --- Machine-local UI state -------------------------------------------------
# Which panes are open, tab order, cursor positions, sidebar widths. Rewritten
# every few seconds and different on every device — the #1 source of pointless
# diffs and merge conflicts when syncing with Obsidian Git.
.obsidian/workspace.json
.obsidian/workspace-mobile.json

# --- Plugin CODE, but not plugin SETTINGS -----------------------------------
# main.js / styles.css / manifest.json are vendored third-party bundles: several
# MB, re-downloaded on every plugin update, and they produce enormous diffs that
# aren't yours. Obsidian re-downloads them when you install the plugin.
#
# data.json is the opposite — that's YOUR config and state: QuickAdd capture
# macros, Templater settings, and Spaced Repetition's flashcard due dates. Lose
# that and you lose real work, so it stays tracked.
#
# community-plugins.json (tracked, above) is the list of what to install, and
# on a fresh clone your data.json is already waiting when you install it.
.obsidian/plugins/*/*
!.obsidian/plugins/*/data.json

# --- Local caches -----------------------------------------------------------
.obsidian/cache
.obsidian/*.cache

# --- Obsidian's own trash ---------------------------------------------------
# Created when "Deleted files → Move to Obsidian trash" is enabled. A local undo
# buffer, not history. (07_Archive/ is your real archive — that IS tracked.)
.trash/

# --- Excalidraw -------------------------------------------------------------
# Your drawings (.excalidraw.md) are content and stay tracked. These two are
# not: a local scratch file and the plugin's bundled font/library cache.
Excalidraw/*.compressed.md
.obsidian/plugins/obsidian-excalidraw-plugin/*.local

# --- OS junk ----------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/

# --- Editor / temp ----------------------------------------------------------
*.swp
*.swo
*~
*.tmp

# =============================================================================
# OPTIONAL — uncomment if they apply to you
# =============================================================================

# Plugin settings that contain secrets. Most data.json files are safe and worth
# committing, but anything talking to an external service stores its API token
# there. Ignore those specific ones — not the whole plugins folder. Examples:
# .obsidian/plugins/readwise-official/data.json
# .obsidian/plugins/obsidian-textgenerator-plugin/data.json
# .obsidian/plugins/obsidian-git/data.json   # if you put a token in the remote URL

# Spaced Repetition rewrites data.json after every review session. If that churn
# bothers you more than losing due dates on a new machine, uncomment:
# .obsidian/plugins/obsidian-spaced-repetition/data.json

# Large binary attachments — if the vault gets heavy, or you'd rather not push
# screenshots and PDFs. Note: notes will then have broken embeds on a fresh clone.
# 08_Meta/Attachments/

# Private folders you don't want on a remote, even a private one.
# 01_Journal/
# 06_People/
